w days he was an impression that if he  didn't bring DJ Durkin back the board  would get rid of him and find someone as  someone else that would some other  president some other interim entity that  would follow their wishes  what's the accused of well they're not  firing him with cause they're not saying  that the program had toxic culture  they're not saying that he was in any  way responsible Jordan McNair his death  I think what they're technically they're  terminating his employment and you don't  need to really explain a reason because  of buying out his contract  so on a very simple level they're just  saying the relationship is not  sustainable DJ Durkin cannot continue as  head coach it's not the best thing for  the team for the university for the  campus community so there they don't  have to outline a specific reason or  give any cause because they don't feel  like they necessarily had cause to fire  him you know just on the spot and  terminate his employment you know what  they'll pay and you guys at the  Washington Post have done a great job  with this story over the last couple of  months but I'm curious with what you saw  with the actual work out where Jordan  McNair died like what what happened  there and who is anybody else culpable  for this aside from DJ Durkin well I  think there's so much finger-pointing  and there's been two investigative  reports and they've outlined a number of  bad actors here and so you know if youse  want to focus on that work out  specifically much of the focus of focus  has been on the athletic trainers who  who failed to diagnose him who failed to  that you know they didn't treat him  because they didn't properly diagnose  them and everyone you talk to would say  that the heatstroke is a very  preventable very curable thing if you  shoot them if you can realize the  temperatures too high get it back down  within an hour and then you know you  just carry on it's treated routinely on  college campuses and elsewhere so the